    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The bag's silhouette resembles a crescent moon, with smooth, rounded lines. This shape is also known as a hobo bag, whose original inspiration comes from the**Bindle**bags carried by late-19th-century American migrant workers—homeless immigrants who wrapped all their belongings in a bundle, hung it on a stick, and carried it over their shoulder as they traveled along train lines in search of work.",
      "url": null,
      "full_url": null,
      "alt": null,
      "columns": 2,
      "items": []
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "text": null,
      "url": "/images/wi7bb4g/9ff0c548d9af3962.jpg",
      "full_url": "/images/wi7bb4g/277f8b4cefa75720.jpg",
      "alt": null,
      "columns": 2,
      "items": []
    },
    {
      "type": "caption",
      "text": "A Bindle bag carried by late-19th-century American migrants",
      "url": null,
      "full_url": null,
      "alt": null,
      "columns": 2,
      "items": []
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "text": null,
      "url": "/images/wi7bb4g/f246127ec9044939.jpg",
      "full_url": "/images/wi7bb4g/1571b2fae8c04e4a.jpg",
      "alt": null,
      "columns": 2,
      "items": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The name \"Hobo\" was borrowed from literature, carrying a semi-heroic American undertone. In his 1930s Great Depression novel *Of Mice and Men*, 20th-century American writer John Steinbeck wrote: \"Thousands of Americans were forced into a hobo lifestyle.\" The world's first hobo bag is said to have been born in 1936, retailing for just 35 cents.",
      "url": null,
      "full_url": null,
      "alt": null,
      "columns": 2,
      "items": []
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "text": null,
